  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "gf_isom_box_parse_ex",
    "file": "gpac/src/isomedia/box_funcs.c",
    "description": "`gf_isom_box_parse_ex` resolves a recognized UUID to an internal type and asks `gf_isom_box_new_ex(uuid_type, parent_type, ...)` for that specialized box. If the specialized UUID type is invalid for the current parent, the factory falls back to a `GF_UnknownBox`. The parser nevertheless checks only the on-wire type (`type == GF_ISOM_BOX_TYPE_UUID`) and unconditionally casts the returned object to `GF_UUIDBox`, then copies 16 UUID bytes and writes `internal_4cc` at lines 331-333. Because `GF_UnknownBox` has a data pointer where `GF_UUIDBox` has its UUID array, this corrupts `GF_UnknownBox::data` with attacker-controlled UUID bytes. The later crash is in `unkn_box_del` when it frees that forged pointer.",
    "conditions": "The input must cause parsing of a UUID box whose UUID maps to a registered specialized internal UUID type, while placing it under a parent not allowed by that type's registry so `gf_isom_box_new_ex` returns a generic `GF_UnknownBox`. In this PoC, a size-zero top-level unknown box of type four spaces consumes the rest of the file; `unkn_box_read` recognizes its payload as a nested box array. The nested box is exactly 24 bytes (`size=24`, type `uuid`, no payload) with UUID `6d1d9b0542d544e680e2141daff757b2`, which resolves to TFXD, but TFXD is only registered for `traf`, not the four-space parent. The UUID copy overwrites the fallback unknown box's `data` pointer, the child remains in the parent's child list, and `gf_isom_close` eventually invokes `unkn_box_del`, which attempts to free the UUID-derived wild address."
  },
